WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.480
- Ann Reardon of How to Cook That

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:02.660
points out that lots of people

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:03.840
would probably hurt themselves

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.270
because of stress viral cooking videos.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.410
And she asked the question.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.020
- Could they see the
sue the platform owner?

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:09.890
- This is a great question,

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.960
and one of the few times
when I can confidently say

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.130
that there is an answer, at
least in the United States.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.430
There's reasons why
YouTube won't be liable

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.560
for people posting really stupid content.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.880
One is the terms of service,
you know that contract

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.810
that you didn't read when
you signed up for YouTube,

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.410
that basically absolves
YouTube of all liability

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.870
related to the videos.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.340
And the second reason is
that infamous section 230

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.150
of the Communications Decency Act,

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:34.200
which provides some protection
for website operators

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.490
and the platforms.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.340
Specifically section
230 prevents a website

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.400
or a platform like YouTube or anyone else

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.400
from having liability for
content posted by somebody else.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.910
And that's probably a good
thing because if YouTube

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:48.440
was responsible for every
stupid thing that a user posted,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.330
well, there probably wouldn't
be too many users on YouTube.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.770
So no YouTube isn't
liable, but theoretically

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.660
those individual users could be,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:57.953
so maybe we'll see them in court.

